# Exhaustiveness: the summary block must be EXACTLY this. Any extra
# application section or changed resource fails here rather than being
# quietly pinned by the next --regenerate.
expect:affected=1 changed=1 resources=+1,-0,~0
# A policy whose CEL expression is broken reports result=error - it never reaches
# pass or fail - and argocdf must SURFACE that, marked, rather than drop it. This
# is the only end-to-end proof of that path: the unit tests feed hand-written JSON
# to the parser, so nothing else shows that real kyverno emits an error result,
# that the adapter forwards it, and that the marker reaches the report.
#
# It matters because a dropped error is invisible by construction. kyverno stops at
# the first failing validation inside a policy, so authors split checks across many
# small policies (see policies/kyverno-broken/broken-expression.yaml) - and one
# broken expression among them simply stops producing findings, with the report
# still reading clean.
must:\[target\] \[kyverno/broken-expression\] ERROR Deployment/cluster-info-web: expression .* resulted in error: no such key: nonexistentField
# Base has no Deployment, so the policy matches nothing there and reports nothing.
must-not:\[base\] \[kyverno/
# The Deployment the policy chokes on really is in the linted input.
must:^\+kind: Deployment
